===Etymology 1===
{{inh+|en|enm|clener}}, {{m|enm|clenere}}, equivalent to {{af|en|clean|-er|id2=agent noun|pos2=[[agent noun]] suffix}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|person who cleans}} A person whose occupation is to clean things, especially rooms, floors, and windows.
#: {{hypo|en|housecleaner|window cleaner<id:person who cleans windows>}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1952|month=February|author=J. Pelham Maitland|title=Locomotive Working on Sussex Branches Fifty Years Ago|journal=Railway Magazine|page=84|text=The '''cleaner''' worked, of course, at nights. He had to coal and light up the engine, as well as clean it, for the next day's work, which commenced with a light run to Barnham to "bring in the goods" from that station at about 6.30 a.m.}}
# {{senseid|en|device that cleans}} A device that [[clean]]s, such as the [[vacuum cleaner]].
# {{senseid|en|substance as cleaning agent}} A [[substance]] used for [[cleaning]]; especially, one [[retail#Verb|retailed]] for that purpose and meant for use on things other than one's own body.
#: {{syn|en|cleaning agent<qq:sometimes hypernymous>}}
#: {{hypo|en|all-purpose cleaner|glass cleaner|scouring powder|window cleaner<id:substance for cleaning windows>}}
#: {{cot|en|soap<!--usually framed as coordinate rather than hyponymous-->|detergent}}
#: {{nearsyn|en|cleaning fluid}}
# {{senseid|en|business establishment}} {{lb|en|in the plural}} A professional [[laundry]] or [[dry cleaner]] {{gloss|business}}.  
#: {{ux|en|I'll have to take this shirt to the '''cleaners'''.}}
# {{lb|en|informal|idiomatic}} A [[fixer]]; a person who disposes of bodies and evidence.

====Usage notes ====
* In regards to sense 5, this form is now interpreted as plural and usually spelled without an apostrophe, even in official usage, to justify the removal of the apostrophe. It was traditionally spelled ''[[cleaner's]]'' with an apostrophe because this is grammatically correct, as can be seen with forms such as ''go to the [[doctor's]]'', which cannot be reinterpreted as plural.

